\chapter{Introduction}

Candlesticks is an interface to a PostgreSQL~\cite{postgresql} database of
historical, time-series financial data.  It stores
\textsc{Ohlc} candlesticks --- open, high, low, close, and an optional
volume --- for any fungible thing that trades in a market: stocks, bonds,
cryptocurrencies, commodities, currencies, indices, and whatever else you
point it at.

A price is always a pair of instruments.  \texttt{BTC/USD} is Bitcoin as
the numerator and the US Dollar as the denominator.
\texttt{AAPL/USD} is Apple stock in dollars.  The same tables hold all of
them.

Every bar knows where it came from.  A \texttt{data\_sources} row names
CoinGecko, Yahoo Finance, Zapper.fi, or whoever else produced the numbers.
A \texttt{data\_retrievals} row records one act of fetching a set of data
from that source --- when it happened, which endpoint was queried, and any
notes.  Each candlestick points at the retrieval that produced it.  The
same numbers from two sources, or from two retrievals of the same source,
are two rows.  That is how we keep multiple instances of the same data and
still know where each one came from.

The long-term plan is for Livermore~\cite{livermore} to take its historical
stock series from this library instead of from
\lispId{livermore/source/stocks.lisp}, and for
\url{https://limbic.fi} to store long-term cryptocurrency histories from
\texttt{cl-coingecko}~\cite{cl-coingecko} the same way.

You can download the library from GitHub at:\\
\url{https://github.com/cgore/candlesticks}

\section*{The Schema}

Tables, all with version-4 \textsc{Uuid} primary keys generated by
PostgreSQL's built-in \texttt{gen\_random\_uuid()} (PostgreSQL~13 or
later).  Schema changes are applied with
\texttt{cl-migratum}~\cite{cl-migratum}; queries go through
Postmodern~\cite{postmodern}.

\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{0.56\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Table & What it holds \\
\midrule
\texttt{instrument\_types} & What an instrument is: stock, cryptocurrency, \ldots \\
\texttt{data\_source\_kinds} & How we talk to a source: api, website, feed, \ldots \\
\texttt{exchanges} & Where it traded: NASDAQ, Binance, Uniswap V3, \ldots \\
\texttt{instruments} & One instrument; identity is the \textsc{Uuid}, not a ticker \\
\texttt{tickers} & Abbreviation a source (and optional exchange) uses for an instrument, with a validity window \\
\texttt{candlestick\_durations} & Short name, long name, and a PostgreSQL interval \\
\texttt{data\_sources} & Who told us: CoinGecko, Yahoo Finance, Zapper.fi, \ldots \\
\texttt{data\_retrievals} & One fetch from a source, optionally pinned to an exchange \\
\texttt{candlesticks} & One \textsc{Ohlc} bar, linked to a pair, a duration, and a retrieval \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

A data source is who told us.  An exchange is where it traded --- a stock
exchange, a \textsc{Cex}, or a \textsc{Dex} (with an optional
\texttt{chain}, so Uniswap V3 on Ethereum is not Uniswap V3 on Arbitrum).
A market in the pair-on-a-venue sense is the bar's numerator and
denominator plus the exchange; that is not a separate table yet.

A ticker is unique as $(\textit{data source}, \textit{canonical
abbreviation}, \textit{exchange})$ \emph{at a given time}.  CoinGecko's
aggregated \texttt{BTC} and Yahoo's \texttt{BTC} on \textsc{Nyse} can
be different instruments.  Yahoo's \texttt{GM} before and after the
2009 bankruptcy can be different instruments too: each ticker row has
a \texttt{valid\_from} / \texttt{valid\_to} window.

The unique key on \texttt{candlesticks} is
$(\textit{numerator}$, $\textit{denominator}$, $\textit{duration}$,
$\textit{time}$, $\textit{retrieval})$.  A check constraint forbids a bar
whose numerator and denominator are the same instrument.

The conventional durations are second, minute, hour, day, week, month, and
year, with short names \texttt{s}, \texttt{m}, \texttt{h}, \texttt{d},
\texttt{w}, \texttt{M}, and \texttt{y}.  Sources that publish other windows
can add their own rows.

Chapter~\ref{ch:schema} walks through every table, column, constraint,
and index.

\section*{Getting Lisp}

Before using this library you need a working Lisp.  I use and recommend
\textsc{Sbcl}, Steel Bank Common Lisp, which is available at:\\
\url{http://www.sbcl.org}

\textsc{SBCL} has information on getting started at:\\
\url{http://www.sbcl.org/getting.html}\\
If you are using Debian or a similar Linux distribution (including Ubuntu), you
can just run as root:\\
\texttt{apt-get install sbcl sbcl-doc sbcl-source}

You also need a PostgreSQL server, version 13 or later.

\section*{Getting \textsc{Emacs} and \textsc{Slime}}

After installing, the best way to interact with any Common Lisp is via
\textsc{Slime}, the Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for \textsc{Emacs}, which is
available at:\\
\url{http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/}\\
This can be installed on Debian by:\\
\texttt{apt-get install slime emacs emacs-goodies-el}

\section*{Using the Library}

Clone the repository next to your other local systems, or symlink the
\textsc{Asdf} file into a directory on \path{asdf:*central-registry*}
(I use \path{~/programming/lisp/systems/}).

\begin{lstlisting}
mkdir -p ~/programming/lisp
cd ~/programming/lisp
git clone git@github.com:cgore/candlesticks.git
ln -s ~/programming/lisp/candlesticks/candlesticks.asd \
      ~/programming/lisp/systems/
\end{lstlisting}

The system depends on $\Sigma$~\cite{sigma}, Postmodern~\cite{postmodern},
\texttt{cl-migratum}, \path{cl-migratum.provider.local-path}, and
\path{cl-migratum.driver.postmodern-postgresql}.  Those come from
Quicklisp (or from a local clone, which is how I load
\texttt{cl-migratum}).

A connection spec is a list that \texttt{postmodern:connect} can be applied
to, for example
\path{("database" "user" "password" "host" :port 5432)}.
\texttt{with-connection} opens one for the duration of its body.  Nesting
or repeating the form gives you several independent connections, which is
how you work against more than one database at a time.

\begin{lstlisting}
(ql:quickload :candlesticks)
(in-package :candlesticks)

(setf *default-connection-spec*
      '("candlesticks" "candlesticks" "secret" "localhost" :port 5432))

(with-connection nil
  (run-migrations *default-connection-spec*)
  (ensure-standard-durations)
  (ensure-standard-instrument-types)
  (ensure-standard-data-source-kinds)
  (ingest-candlesticks "BTC" "USD" "d"
                       (list (list (encode-universal-time 0 0 0 18 8 2026 0)
                                   25000.0 26000.0 24000.0 25500.0 100.0))
                       :source-name "CoinGecko"
                       :source-kind "api"
                       :numerator-name "Bitcoin"
                       :numerator-type "cryptocurrency"
                       :denominator-name "US Dollar"
                       :denominator-type "currency")
  (candlestick-close-prices (get-candlesticks "BTC" "USD" "d")))
\end{lstlisting}

Times are Common Lisp universal times.  Postmodern hands
\texttt{timestamptz} values back as universal times, and
\texttt{numeric} values as rationals; the library converts the latter to
floats on the way out.

\section*{Testing}

Specs are \texttt{sigma/behave} \texttt{behavior} / \texttt{should} forms,
the same style as $\Sigma$.  Pure helpers run at load time.
\texttt{asdf:test-system} reloads the sources so those assertions run
again.

\begin{lstlisting}
(asdf:test-system :candlesticks)
\end{lstlisting}

To also hit a live PostgreSQL server, set two environment variables and
run \texttt{asdf:test-system} again.

\begin{lstlisting}
CANDLESTICKS_LIVE_TESTS=1
CANDLESTICKS_LIVE_DB="database user password host port"
\end{lstlisting}

The live suite creates a throwaway schema, runs the migrations, exercises
the whole \textsc{Api} --- including provenance, duplicate ingestion, and
nested connections --- and drops the schema when it is done.

Have fun!
